WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.462
Plane-mirror questions look simple, but they quietly combine two different ideas.

2
00:00:05.462 --> 00:00:10.925
One idea is reflection geometry.

3
00:00:10.925 --> 00:00:16.388
The incoming and outgoing light routes make equal angles around the Guide Line, also called the normal.

4
00:00:16.388 --> 00:00:21.850
The second idea is image position.

5
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:27.312
The plane-mirror image appears behind the mirror even though no real light is travelling there.

6
00:00:27.312 --> 00:00:32.775
Students often mix those two stories or measure angles from the surface instead of the Guide Line.

7
00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:38.238
The safest way to enter the worked example is to keep the geometry rule and the ghost-image rule in two separate slots and then connect them carefully.

8
00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:43.700
Keep this takeaway in view: Mirror questions become calmer when Guide-Line geometry and ghost-image reasoning stay separate.

9
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.162
Geometry slot means equal angles.

10
00:00:49.162 --> 00:00:54.625
Reflection uses the Guide Line as the angle reference.

11
00:00:54.625 --> 00:01:00.087
Image slot means ghost image.

12
00:01:00.087 --> 00:01:05.550
The image behind the mirror comes from backward extensions, not real light.

13
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.764
This diagram earns its place because it holds both mirror stories at once without mixing them.

14
00:01:09.764 --> 00:01:13.977
The incoming route approaches the mirror.

15
00:01:13.977 --> 00:01:18.191
The Guide Line is drawn at right angles to the surface.

16
00:01:18.191 --> 00:01:22.404
The reflected route leaves with a matching angle to that same Guide Line.

17
00:01:22.404 --> 00:01:26.618
That is the geometry story.

18
00:01:26.618 --> 00:01:30.831
But the image behind the mirror is different.

19
00:01:30.831 --> 00:01:35.045
It is not where real light is travelling.

20
00:01:35.045 --> 00:01:39.259
It is where dashed backward extensions appear to meet.

21
00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:43.472
Reading the diagram in two passes is the best habit.

22
00:01:43.472 --> 00:01:47.686
First read the equal-angle reflection.

23
00:01:47.686 --> 00:01:51.899
Then read the virtual image position.

24
00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:56.113
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong mirror answer reads reflection geometry first and image position second.

25
00:01:56.113 --> 00:02:00.326
Guide Line: Use the perpendicular reference line for every reflection angle.

26
00:02:00.326 --> 00:02:04.540
Reflected route: Copy the incident angle around the same Guide Line.

27
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:11.289
Imagine tossing a soft ball toward a smooth hallway panel and also seeing your own reflection in it.

28
00:02:11.289 --> 00:02:18.038
The ball analogy helps with the equal-angle bounce, while the reflection of your own face helps with the apparent image behind the panel.

29
00:02:18.038 --> 00:02:24.787
These are not the same effect, but they share one optical surface.

30
00:02:24.787 --> 00:02:31.536
That is why plane-mirror questions can feel heavier than they first appear.

31
00:02:31.536 --> 00:02:38.285
The lesson becomes much easier once you stop expecting one single rule to explain both the bounce geometry and the image position at the same time.

32
00:02:38.285 --> 00:02:45.034
Keep this takeaway in view: Plane-mirror questions become clearer when the bounce rule and the image rule are handled separately.

33
00:02:45.034 --> 00:02:51.783
Bounce idea means route symmetry.

34
00:02:51.783 --> 00:02:58.532
A smooth surface gives a clean reflected direction around the Guide Line.

35
00:02:58.532 --> 00:03:05.281
Appearance idea means object behind panel.

36
00:03:05.281 --> 00:03:12.030
The reflected face seems to be behind the surface even though the light is in front.

37
00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:17.697
The first core statement is angle of incidence equals angle of reflection.

38
00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:23.364
The key phrase is that both angles are measured from the Guide Line or normal, not from the mirror surface.

39
00:03:23.364 --> 00:03:29.031
The second core statement is object distance equals image distance for a plane mirror.

40
00:03:29.031 --> 00:03:34.698
That means the virtual image sits the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front.

41
00:03:34.698 --> 00:03:40.365
These are the two rules that power most introductory mirror questions.

42
00:03:40.365 --> 00:03:46.032
The trap is not that students forget the rules.

43
00:03:46.032 --> 00:03:51.698
It is that they use the wrong reference line or mix up real and apparent positions.

44
00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:57.365
Keep this takeaway in view: Mirror success depends on the right reference line and the right image status, not on memory alone.

45
00:03:57.365 --> 00:04:03.032
Rule one: Use the same Guide Line on both sides of the reflection.

46
00:04:03.032 --> 00:04:08.699
Rule two: Place the image the same distance behind the mirror.

47
00:04:08.699 --> 00:04:14.366
angle of incidence = angle of reflection.

48
00:04:14.366 --> 00:04:20.033
measure from the Guide Line or normal, not from the surface.

49
00:04:20.033 --> 00:04:25.700
If the question gives an angle to the surface, convert it before applying the equal-angle rule.

50
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:31.214
The first common trap is the surface angle.

51
00:04:31.214 --> 00:04:36.728
If a question says the route makes twenty degrees with the mirror surface, that is not yet the incidence angle used in the rule.

52
00:04:36.728 --> 00:04:42.242
Because the Guide Line is perpendicular to the surface, the Guide-Line angle is ninety minus twenty, which is seventy degrees.

53
00:04:42.242 --> 00:04:47.755
The second common trap is image status.

54
00:04:47.755 --> 00:04:53.269
Students sometimes think the mirror image is a real object behind the mirror.

55
00:04:53.269 --> 00:04:58.783
It is not.

56
00:04:58.783 --> 00:05:04.297
The image position is where the reflected routes appear to come from when extended backward.

57
00:05:04.297 --> 00:05:09.811
Those two traps are exactly why this lesson deserves a rich pre-worked explanation.

58
00:05:09.811 --> 00:05:15.325
Keep this takeaway in view: Mirror diagrams stop being slippery when every angle and every line is given the correct role.

59
00:05:15.325 --> 00:05:20.838
Trap one means surface angle.

60
00:05:20.838 --> 00:05:26.352
Convert to the Guide Line before using the reflection rule.

61
00:05:26.352 --> 00:05:31.866
Trap two means real versus apparent.

62
00:05:31.866 --> 00:05:37.380
The plane-mirror image is an apparent position, not a real crossing of light.

63
00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.717
Try four checks.

64
00:05:40.717 --> 00:05:44.053
From which line are reflection angles measured?

65
00:05:44.053 --> 00:05:47.390
The Guide Line or normal.

66
00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:50.727
If a route is thirty degrees to the surface, what is it to the normal?

67
00:05:50.727 --> 00:05:54.063
Sixty degrees.

68
00:05:54.063 --> 00:05:57.400
If the object is two metres in front of the mirror, where is the image?

69
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.737
Two metres behind it.

70
00:06:00.737 --> 00:06:04.073
And are the dashed image lines real beams?

71
00:06:04.073 --> 00:06:07.410
No, they are backward extensions used to locate the ghost image.

72
00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.747
Those four checks capture almost the whole lesson.

73
00:06:10.747 --> 00:06:14.083
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the mirror routine is now stable and diagram-first.

74
00:06:14.083 --> 00:06:17.420
Reference means Guide Line.

75
00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:20.757
Keep this line in charge of the angle rule.

76
00:06:20.757 --> 00:06:24.093
Conversion means 90 minus surface angle.

77
00:06:24.093 --> 00:06:27.430
Use this only when the question gives the wrong reference line first.

78
00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:33.582
You are ready for the worked example now.

79
00:06:33.582 --> 00:06:39.735
Start by asking what the question is really testing.

80
00:06:39.735 --> 00:06:45.887
If it is about route direction, draw the Guide Line and apply the equal-angle rule using the correct reference.

81
00:06:45.887 --> 00:06:52.039
If it gives a surface angle, convert it first.

82
00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:58.192
If it is about image location, place the image the same distance behind the plane mirror as the object is in front and remember that the image is virtual.

83
00:06:58.192 --> 00:07:04.344
If both stories appear in one question, handle them in two passes.

84
00:07:04.344 --> 00:07:10.496
That is the cleanest way into the worked example.

85
00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:16.648
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: mirror geometry and plane-mirror image reasoning now have clear roles.

86
00:07:16.648 --> 00:07:22.801
Step 1: Decide whether the example is testing the route, the image, or both.

87
00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:28.953
Step 2: Measure or convert the angle relative to the Guide Line.

88
00:07:28.953 --> 00:07:35.105
identify whether the question is about route direction, image position, or both.

89
00:07:35.105 --> 00:07:41.258
use the Guide Line for all reflection angles and convert any surface angle first.

90
00:07:41.258 --> 00:07:47.410
The best mirror answers protect the reference line first and the image status second.
